{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfwUtAYyRLjgmCRrGypk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I get **Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 618 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 2490 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 4.0)**. Dq and B come straight from the spin-allowed bands. C comes from the spin-forbidden band, using a ²E matrix that I wrote from memory, so it is the least certain of the three.\n\n**1. Dq from the first band**\n\nFor a d³ ion in an octahedral field, the lowest spin-allowed band is ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g, and its energy equals 10Dq.\n- 10Dq = 17 500 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 1750 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. B from the other two spin-allowed bands**\n\nThe other two bands are ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(F) at 23 860 cm⁻¹ and ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(P) at 37 910 cm⁻¹. The two ⁴T₁g states mix through a 2×2 matrix, which gives two rules:\n- The two band energies add up to 30Dq + 15B. So 61 770 = 52 500 + 15B, which gives **B ≈ 618 cm⁻¹**.\n- As a check, the gap between them should be √[(10Dq − 9B)² + 144B²]. With these values that is about 14 054 cm⁻¹, against 14 050 cm⁻¹ observed. The fit is consistent.\n\n**3. C from the sharp band at 12 090 cm⁻¹**\n\nThe weak, sharp band is ⁴A₂g → ²Eg.\n- **Simple formula first:** the usual approximation E(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/(10Dq) gives C ≈ 2830 cm⁻¹. That formula treats the mixing between ²E states only to second order and ignores their electron-repulsion (B, C) terms, so it overestimates C here.\n- **Full calculation:** I diagonalised the full 4×4 ²E matrix, which covers the t₂³, t₂²(¹E)e, t₂²(¹A₁)e and e³ states, using the Tanabe–Sugano matrix elements. I adjusted C until the lowest ²E level sat 12 090 cm⁻¹ above ⁴A₂. This gives **C ≈ 2490 cm⁻¹**.\n- **How reliable that is:**\n  - The diagonal terms pass a sum (trace) check against the free-ion energies.\n  - The two matrix elements that couple t₂³ to the t₂²e states reproduce the 90B²/(10Dq) term of the simple formula.\n  - I could not check the remaining matrix elements, but they only shift the lowest ²E level slightly.\n  - An empirical formula from the literature (I'm quoting it from memory), E(²E) ≈ 3.05C + 7.90B − 1.80B²/Dq, gives about 2492 cm⁻¹, which agrees.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1750, \"B\": 618, \"C\": 2490, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"10Dq from the first band, B from the sum of the two 4T1 bands, and C from fitting the lowest 2E level with a 2E matrix recalled from memory.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":857,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":857,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa2444b7-460e-4529-ac01-aa6a7a277d1e","uuid":"0d691f92-91b9-47e5-9652-cef335b30a60","timestamp":"2026-11-03T11:47:51.370Z","request_id":"req_011CgfwUsqwYykdNmJov9HpN"}
